Recent advancements in generative adversarial networks have led to the development of conditional GANs, which have been successfully applied to various tasks, including financial time series analysis. However, these models are limited by their reliance on a fixed latent space, which can hinder their ability to capture the complex patterns and relationships within financial data. To address this limitation, we propose a novel approach that utilizes a dynamic latent space to capture the temporal dependencies in financial time series. Our method, which we call the Dynamic Conditional GAN, incorporates a dynamic latent space that evolves over time, allowing it to capture the changing patterns and relationships within financial data. We evaluate the performance of our approach using a dataset of S\&P 500 stock prices and compare it to traditional conditional GANs. Our results show that the Dynamic Conditional GAN outperforms the traditional approach in terms of both predictive accuracy and interpretability. Additionally, we demonstrate that our approach can be used to identify and analyze the underlying factors that drive financial time series, providing valuable insights for investors and financial analysts. Our code is publicly available on GitHub. \end{abstract} 

